3.4. Pre-processing
The pre-processing stage (S3 of Figure 1) takes as input the emails’ body text (i.e, the first column of E) and aims the elimination of redundant and trivial information. Furthermore, it contributes to the creation of a consistent format for all the emails’ text. The pre-processing of the text is a common procedure that has been applied in many text classification tasks and can be found in several previous works (e.g., [27]). This stage aims to assist the textual feature extraction stage identifying the words of the email corpus that are more informative. The actions that pre-processing performs to accomplish its goals are presented in Figure 2. The pre-processing begins by converting the words into lowercase. Next, it removes all the stop words, special characters, punctuation marks, and HTML elements (when they exist in an email). The hyperlinks that may appear in the email’s body text are replaced with a fixed string (e.g., LINKLINK). The reason behind using a fixed string and not a common word, like “URL” or “LINK”, is that the common word may appear in the text, hence the results will be falsified. Later, a tokenization process is performed [24], where a delimiter is used to split the words in order to convert the text

